  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 309 AM EDT Wed Aug 12 2026\n\n***** STORM CHANCES WANE, HEAT BUILDS INTO THE WEEKEND *****\n\nGlobal models and their ensembles agree that ridging will slowly \nbuild over the area into the weekend. One or more additional mid-\nlevel disturbances will offer thunderstorm chances Thursday into \nFriday, mainly east of I-65, but then the MCS corridor should shift \nto the northeast by Saturday. This will allow the heat to intensify \nacross the area over the weekend, with triple digit heat indices, \nconfined to areas mainly west of I-65 today and tomorrow, expanding \neast to encompass the entire area both Saturday and Sunday. In fact, \nthere is a 60-90% (20-40%) chance HeatRisk reaches the major \n(extreme) category by Sunday afternoon - suggesting an increased risk \nfor heat-related illness, especially among vulnerable populations \nincluding those without proper cooling.\n\nA shortwave trough should flatten the ridge and drop a frontal \nboundary over the area sometime early next work week, potentially \noffering some relief from the heat for at least a portion of the \narea and serving as a trigger for showers and storms. It's too early \nto tell whether a severe weather risk may materialize with this \nactivity, but the joint probability of > 25 kt 0-6km bulk shear and \n> 2000 J/kg MUCAPE from the LREF is less than 20%, which, at least \nat this point in time, suggests the risk should be relatively low. \nGiven the richly moist and unstable airmass ahead of the trough and \nattendant front, heavy rain and localized microbursts in water-laden \ncollapsing cores would seem to be the primary hazards (if any).",
